# Agentic workflow guardrails

Principle: manual where the user thinks, agentic where it builds. The user owns the what (spec, design,
outline, final code read); you own the how (research, implement, test, self-review); tests decide when a
slice is done.

- **Artifacts live in `.workflow/`.** The active work item is in `.workflow/active.env`
  (`WORK_DIR`, `TRACK` feature|bug|small, `PHASE` start|research|brainstorm|plan|build|pr|escalated,
  `SLICE`, `LOCK_SHA`). Read it before acting on any `/wf-*` command. Write it as plain `KEY=value` lines.
- **Never edit, delete, skip or loosen a locked test** (files in `$WORK_DIR/locked-tests.txt`). If a locked
  test looks wrong, stop and run `/wf-escalate`. Fix the code, not the tests.
- **Respect the limits** in `.workflow/config.env`: stop-hook runs `MAX_STOP_RUNS` (5), slice review rounds
  `MAX_REVIEW_ROUNDS` (3), PR fix rounds `MAX_PR_FIX_ROUNDS` (2). On a limit: `/wf-escalate`. Never "one more try".
- **Plan issues escalate; code issues get fixed.** If the outline, design or a requirement is wrong, do not
  improvise a new plan inside a build or PR loop: run `/wf-escalate`.
- **The repo's CI defines passing.** Before show-me and before the PR, run the repo's own CI checks locally
  (`CI_CMD`, e.g. `make ci`, or the checks found in the Makefile / `.github/workflows/`, cached in
  `.workflow/ci-checks.md`; see `.workflow/kit/ci-procedure.md`). Never silence or weaken a check to get green.
- **Evidence before claims.** Never say tests pass, a bug is fixed or a slice is done without fresh command
  output from this turn.
- **Stay inside the current slice.** Touch only what its acceptance criteria need; no drive-by refactors.
- **Traceability.** Keep requirement / hazard IDs on slices, tests, commits and the PR description.
- **Never** merge, push, force-push, `reset --hard`, `clean`, or delete branches without the user asking
  for that exact action in this conversation.
- **Repo setup comes first.** The per-repo files live in `.workflow/config.env` and `.workflow/kit/`
  (scaling, CI procedure, templates). If they are missing, run the setup step of `/wf-start` before any
  other `/wf-*` skill.
- **Chat titles.** Every chat is named `<topic> · /wf-<stage>` so open chats are easy to tell apart.
  `<topic>` is the current git branch when it is a feature branch (not `main`, `master`, `develop` or a
  detached HEAD); otherwise the work-item folder name from `WORK_DIR` (e.g. `2026-11-20-network-probe`);
  before a work item exists, the topic in a few words. Examples: `feat/network-probe · /wf-build`,
  `2026-11-20-network-probe · /wf-pr`.
  - When a stage starts, name the chat it runs in: use the chat-rename tool if you have one (Cursor:
    `rename_chat`). If you do not, print one line for the user: `Name this chat: <title>` (Claude Code:
    `/rename <title>`). Rename only when the stage changes; the next slice in `/wf-build` keeps its title.
  - A hand-off that recommends a new chat proposes that chat's title with its stage (see below).
- **Hand-off: recommend, ask, then continue.** Every `/wf-*` skill ends by updating `PHASE`, saying where
  to run the next step, and asking "Continue with `/wf-<next>` now?". On a yes, read
  the `/wf-<next>` skill and follow it in this chat. On a no (or no answer), stop; the user can
  run the command later. Never continue without a yes. Where to run the next step (every artifact is on
  disk, so a new chat loses nothing but the conversation):
  | Next step | Recommend |
  |---|---|
  | `/wf-research`, `/wf-brainstorm`, `/wf-plan` | Continue here (short, conversational stages) |
  | `/wf-build` (first slice, from plan or debug) | **New chat**, then `/wf-build`: the outline is all it needs |
  | Next slice in `/wf-build` | Continue here if the chat is short; **new chat** if it is long or the slice was large |
  | `/wf-pr` | **New chat**, then `/wf-pr`: it reviews the whole branch from files |
  | After `/wf-escalate` | Continue here if short; otherwise **new chat**, the reason is in `decisions.md` |
  Say it as one line with the new chat's title, e.g. "Recommended: start a new chat named
  `feat/network-probe · /wf-build` and run `/wf-build` (keeps the build's context clean). Or continue here now?"
